def stamp(x,cids):x.update(claims_seen={c:1 for c in cids},qa_status='review_required');x['authored_hash']=hashlib.sha256(_artefact_text(x).encode()).hexdigest()

# Each pair is a separately written proposition supporting the exact objective.
FACTS={
'01':['The three main compositional layers from outside inward are crust, mantle and core.','Crust and uppermost rigid mantle together form lithosphere; the compositional and mechanical divisions differ.'],
'01-01':['Crust is the thin solid outer rock layer, with generally thicker and less dense continental crust than oceanic crust.','In the supplied crust example, 8 km and 3.0 g/cm³ contrast with 38 km and 2.7 g/cm³; the former is thinner and denser.'],
'01-02':['The mantle consists predominantly of solid hot rock that can deform slowly over geological time.','Evidence of slow flow does not establish that the entire mantle is molten.'],
'01-03':['The core is the central metallic region, with a liquid outer part and a solid inner part.','High pressure helps explain why the hotter inner core remains solid; temperature alone does not determine state.'],
'02':['A tectonic plate is a relatively rigid moving section of lithosphere.','Plate tectonics explains plate movements and interactions; a plate is an object, whereas plate tectonics is the explanatory framework.'],
'03':['Mantle convection involves slow circulation associated with hotter, less dense material rising and cooler, denser material sinking.','Convection is connected to plate motion; sinking slabs and ridge-related forces also contribute, rather than every plate being dragged by an identical circular current.'],
'04':['Convergent margins bring plates together, divergent margins separate them, and conservative margins involve sideways sliding.','Subduction consumes oceanic lithosphere, constructive spreading creates crust, and conservative movement does neither systematically.'],
'04-01':['An oceanic slab can descend beneath another plate at a destructive margin, forming a trench near its bend.','Volcanoes above subduction generally lie on the overriding side, while earthquakes occur along the interface and descending slab.'],
'04-02':['Constructive margins have diverging plates, rising magma and solidification of new crust near the opening.','A broadly symmetrical increase in seafloor ages away from a central line supports seafloor spreading.'],
'04-03':['Conservative margins involve horizontal relative movement parallel to the margin.','Frictional locking followed by sudden slip can cause earthquakes without systematic creation or consumption of lithosphere.'],
'05':['Fold mountains, trenches, island arcs and mid-ocean ridges occur in belts associated with particular plate interactions.','A resource distribution answer describes pattern and spatial relationships; a formation answer explains the processes producing them.'],
'05-01':['Fold-mountain belts occur in compressional convergent settings, including continental collision.','A belt following squares A2, B2 and C3 can be described by its continuous alignment and change in direction, without inventing a country.'],
'05-02':['Deep-sea trenches are narrow elongated ocean-floor depressions at subduction zones.','A 7,500 m deep strip beside a 4,000 m ocean floor is 3,500 m deeper than that floor, but remains 7,500 m below sea level.'],
'05-03':['Volcanic island arcs commonly lie on the overriding side of ocean–ocean subduction systems and broadly parallel their trenches.','A curved island chain alone does not prove subduction; an associated trench and convergent movement strengthen the interpretation.'],
'05-04':['Mid-ocean ridges form submarine mountain belts at divergent margins where new oceanic crust is produced.','Crust ages increasing from 0 through 10 to 20 million years away from both sides of a central line support spreading from that line.'],
'06':['Compression shortens and thickens crust and can fold sedimentary layers.','Continental collision can produce folding, faulting, crustal thickening and uplift, as in the Himalayas.'],
'07':['Ash-and-cinder cones are commonly steep fragment-built cones; shields are broad and gently sloping; stratovolcanoes are composite cones.','Volcano shape should be interpreted with scale and material evidence, rather than assuming height alone proves the type.'],
'07-01':['Ash-and-cinder cones accumulate loose erupted fragments around a vent and commonly have a distinct summit crater.','A crater alone is not diagnostic because it can occur on different volcano types.'],
'07-02':['Shield or lava volcanoes are broad with gentle sides, associated with repeated relatively fluid lava flows.','Gentle slopes do not establish absence of hazards: lava and gas can still affect people and infrastructure.'],
'07-03':['Stratovolcanoes commonly have relatively steep cones built from lava and fragmented deposits accumulated over repeated eruptions.','Mayon is a stratovolcano; the 20 January 2018 bulletin reported lava effusion and collapse-related activity, showing that composite cones can produce more than one hazard.'],
'08':['Many earthquakes and volcanoes cluster near plate margins, including much of the Pacific margin system.','Earthquakes and hotspot volcanism also occur within plates, so the distribution is not exclusively on margins.'],
'09':['Earthquakes result from sudden fault movement releasing stored strain energy as seismic waves.','Volcanic eruptions bring magma and gases to the surface and can produce lava, ash, pyroclastic flows and subsequent lahars.'],
'09-01':['The focus is where earthquake rupture begins; the epicentre lies on the surface vertically above it.','The 11 March 2011 Tohoku earthquake has a current USGS magnitude of 9.1 and generated a destructive tsunami affecting northeastern Honshu.'],
'09-02':['Magma below the surface is called lava once it emerges; pyroclastic flows and lahars are not the same as lava flows.','The Mayon 20 January 2018 bulletin described advancing lava, rockfalls and ash clouds; its restrictions were dated advice, not a timeless safe-distance rule.'],
'10':['Hazard management reduces vulnerability or exposure through measures such as construction standards, monitoring, evacuation and emergency provision.','A judgement about management needs a specified danger, time scale and exposed group; no single measure addresses every hazard.'],
'10-01':['Retrofitting a weak school directly targets collapse risk, while warning messages alone do not strengthen its walls.','Earthquake-resistant construction does not automatically protect occupants from tsunami inundation.'],
'10-02':['Volcano monitoring informs evacuation decisions but does not prevent the eruption.','For people inside a threatened area, effective evacuation depends on usable routes, transport, communication and shelter.'],
}
