CARDS=[
('Name the thin, solid rock layer at the Earth’s surface.','Crust.',['1 mark for the name crust.']),
('Describe the arrangement of R, S and T from this record: R is the outer rock layer; S is the thick rocky region immediately beneath R; T is the central metallic region.','R surrounds S, while S surrounds T. R is crust, S is mantle and T is core. The sequence inward is crust → mantle → core.',['1 mark for the outer crust position, 1 for mantle beneath crust, and 1 for the central core relationship; use the given record as evidence.']),
('Describe the differences between crust at A and B using the data. A: thickness 8 km, density 3.0 g/cm³. B: thickness 38 km, density 2.7 g/cm³.','A is thinner: 8 km rather than 38 km, a difference of 30 km. A is also denser: 3.0 rather than 2.7 g/cm³. The paired pattern is consistent with oceanic crust at A and continental crust at B.',['1 mark for the thickness comparison with numerical evidence; 1 for the density comparison with values; 1 for the interpretation of the combined feature pattern as oceanic A and continental B.']),
('Describe what this material record indicates about mantle behaviour: “Predominantly solid hot rock; gradual deformation under sustained stress over geological time.”','The mantle is largely solid rock, not a global liquid layer. The record nevertheless indicates slow deformation. Its ability to flow depends on the long time scale, so slow flow does not demonstrate complete melting.',['1 mark for the solid-rock feature; 1 for gradual deformation from the observation; 1 for connecting flow to the long time scale without calling the entire mantle molten.']),
('Describe the structure of central region T from the record: “Mainly metallic; outer part liquid; inner part solid.”','T is the metallic core. Its liquid outer core surrounds a solid inner core; it is not uniformly liquid throughout.',['1 mark for metallic composition as evidence; 1 for the outer liquid feature; 1 for the inner solid feature and correct internal arrangement.']),
('Distinguish between a tectonic plate and plate tectonics.','A plate is a relatively rigid section of lithosphere containing crust and uppermost mantle. Plate tectonics is the explanation of how plates move and interact to produce tectonic features and hazards.',['1 mark for a plate as a lithospheric section; 1 for plate tectonics as the movement/interaction explanation; 1 for making the object-versus-explanation difference explicit.']),
('Suggest how these observations distinguish a moving plate from the explanation called plate tectonics: repeated surveys show marks on one rigid slab moving together, while marks across its edge become farther apart.','Marks moving together support a relatively rigid moving plate. Increasing separation across its edge supports divergence between plates. Plate tectonics links that given motion to interactions such as spreading and formation of new crust.',['1 mark for applying coherent movement as evidence of a plate; 1 for increasing separation as evidence of divergence; 1 for connecting the observations to the broader plate-tectonic explanation, with a reason.']),
('Describe the sequence of mantle convection associated with plate movement.','Hotter, less dense mantle material rises slowly. It spreads and transfers heat nearer the lithosphere. Cooler, denser material sinks elsewhere, completing circulation connected to plate motion.',['1 mark for the first upward step and its density relationship; 1 for spreading and heat transfer; 1 for sinking as the result of cooling/density change. Do not require a claim that the whole mantle is liquid.']),
('Describe the circulation shown by this data record: A = hot material rises; B = flow turns sideways beneath the lithosphere; C = cooler material sinks. The surface arrows above A point apart.','The A–B–C sequence shows upward flow followed by sideways spreading and downward return. The surface plates diverge above A. The flow pattern is consistent with convection associated with a constructive setting.',['1 mark for the rising-to-sideways flow pattern; 1 for the cooler downward return shown by the data; 1 for relating the apart-pointing surface arrows to divergence.']),
('Distinguish constructive, destructive and conservative plate margins by their relative movements.','Constructive margins have plates moving apart; destructive subduction margins have plates converging with one descending; conservative margins have plates sliding sideways past one another.',['1 mark per correctly contrasted movement: constructive apart, destructive convergence with descent, conservative sideways. Include each difference rather than repeating that all plates move.']),
('Suggest which margin is consistent with the evidence and give two reasons: a survey shows two plates separating, and the youngest oceanic crust occurs along the gap.','A constructive margin is consistent with the evidence. Increasing separation indicates divergence, while youngest crust at the gap supports creation of new crust there.',['1 mark for constructive identification; 1 for the given separation as a reason; 1 for youngest crust at the gap as evidence of new-crust formation.']),
('Describe the movement and arrangement in the supplied destructive-margin cross-section, using its arrows, slab and trench.','The plates converge. The oceanic slab bends down at the trench and descends beneath the overriding plate; the volcanoes lie on the overriding side, away from the trench.',['1 mark for converging movement pattern shown by the arrows; 1 for the slab descending from the trench; 1 for the overriding-side volcanic position as diagram evidence.']),
('Describe crust formation in the supplied constructive-margin cross-section, using its arrows and central opening.','The arrows show plates moving apart. Magma rises into the opening and cools there. Newly solidified crust forms at the centre and moves away with the diverging plates.',['1 mark for apart-pointing movement as diagram evidence; 1 for magma rising and cooling at the central opening; 1 for newly formed crust and its outward pattern.']),
('Describe relative movement in the supplied conservative-margin plan view, using its arrows and offset stream.','The plates slide horizontally past one another parallel to the margin. The opposed arrows show their relative directions. The stream’s sideways offset is consistent with displacement along the fault.',['1 mark for the sideways movement pattern; 1 for the opposed parallel-arrow evidence; 1 for relating the offset feature to lateral displacement.']),
('Name the tectonic landform consisting of a long, narrow ocean-floor depression at a subduction margin.','A deep-sea trench.',['1 mark for the name deep-sea trench or ocean trench.']),
('Describe the relationship in this resource: a convergent margin follows a curved line; a deep trench lies along its oceanward side; volcanic islands form a chain roughly parallel to it on the overriding side.','The trench follows the convergent margin as a curved belt. The volcanic islands form a parallel chain. They lie on the overriding side, rather than in the trench itself.',['1 mark for the trench–margin alignment pattern; 1 for the parallel island-chain evidence; 1 for the correct relative position on the overriding side.']),
('Describe the distribution of the mountain belt in this teaching map record: connected mountain shading crosses A2 → B2 → C3 beside a convergent margin. Columns A–C run west to east; row 3 is south of row 2.','The mountains form a connected belt rather than isolated scattered areas. It trends east from A2 to B2 and then southeast to C3. Its alignment follows the convergent margin.',['1 mark for the connected belt pattern; 1 for east then southeast using the supplied grid evidence; 1 for alignment with the margin.']),
('Describe the trench evidence in this depth transect: surrounding floor 4,000 m below sea level; narrow strip 7,500 m; floor beyond 3,000 m. The strip follows a convergent margin.','The strip is much deeper than the floor on either side, reaching 7,500 m below sea level. It is 3,500 m deeper than the 4,000 m floor. Its narrow form and margin-parallel position are consistent with a trench.',['1 mark for the deep narrow feature with 7,500 m evidence; 1 for the correct 3,500 m depth difference; 1 for the relationship to the convergent margin.']),
('Describe how the supplied pattern supports a volcanic island arc: a curved island chain lies on the overriding side, parallel to a trench, and plate arrows converge.','The islands form a curved chain rather than a random scatter. Their parallel position on the overriding side matches an arc above subduction. The trench and converging arrows provide additional evidence for that interpretation.',['1 mark for the curved-chain pattern; 1 for the overriding-side parallel position; 1 for using trench/convergence data to support subduction rather than relying on curvature alone.']),
('Describe the crust-age pattern across this teaching ridge: west outer 20 Ma, west inner 10 Ma, centre 0 Ma, east inner 10 Ma, east outer 20 Ma. Ma means million years.','The youngest crust is at the centre. Ages increase away from it on both sides, from 0 to 10 to 20 Ma. The paired pattern is consistent with new crust forming at a ridge and moving outward.',['1 mark for youngest central crust; 1 for the bilateral age-increase pattern with values; 1 for connecting that evidence to spreading.']),
('Describe the sequence by which continental convergence can form fold mountains.','Continental convergence compresses the crust. Layers buckle and crust is shortened and thickened. Uplift produces a mountain belt, later modified by erosion.',['1 mark for the first compression step; 1 for folding with shortening/thickening; 1 for uplift as the mountain-building result.']),
('Describe the formation sequence from this record: A = flat layers between approaching continents; B = the layers buckle as horizontal width decreases; C = thickened crust and higher relief.','The approaching continents compress the layers. Their reduced width and buckling show shortening and folding at B. Thickening and uplift produce the higher relief shown at C.',['1 mark for compression feature supported by A’s evidence; 1 for linking B’s observed buckling/reduced width to folding/shortening; 1 for the C thickening–uplift result.']),
('Name the broad volcano type built mainly by repeated flows of relatively fluid lava, with gentle slopes.','A shield volcano, also called a lava volcano.',['1 mark for the name shield volcano or lava volcano.']),
('Describe the main shape contrasts in this original data record: A = steep small cone of loose cinders; B = very broad gentle cone built mainly of lava flows; C = taller steep cone with lava and ash layers.','A is a steep fragment-built cinder cone. B has a broader, gentler shield profile. C is a steep composite cone with visible layering, consistent with a stratovolcano.',['1 mark for A’s steep fragment-built feature using the resource evidence; 1 for B’s broad gentle contrast; 1 for C’s layered composite profile, using the given observations.']),
('Describe the evidence for an ash-and-cinder cone in this record: loose fragments cluster around one vent, the sides are steep, and a bowl-like crater occupies the summit.','The fragments are concentrated around the vent. They build steep sides and a summit crater. Together these observed features are consistent with a cinder cone; the crater alone would not settle the type.',['1 mark for the fragment-around-vent pattern; 1 for steep sides and crater from the observations; 1 for interpreting the combined evidence without treating a crater alone as diagnostic.']),
('Describe the profile indicated by these teaching dimensions: volcano B is 1,000 m high and 20 km wide at its base; it is built mainly of extensive fluid-lava flows.','B is broad relative to its height. Each half-base is 10,000 m, giving a height/half-width ratio of 0.1, consistent with gentle average sides. The extensive lava-flow evidence supports a shield interpretation.',['1 mark for the broad-relative-to-height feature; 1 for the correctly interpreted 0.1 ratio using consistent units; 1 for linking the flow evidence to a shield profile.']),
('Describe the evidence for a stratovolcano: cone C is relatively steep, with exposed deposits of lava and fragmented ash from repeated eruptions.','C has a relatively steep cone. Its exposed lava and ash layers show composite construction. Repeated deposition of different erupted materials supports identification as a stratovolcano.',['1 mark for the steep-profile feature; 1 for the lava/ash layering observation; 1 for relating repeated composite deposition to the classification.']),
('Name the broad Pacific-margin belt associated with many earthquakes and volcanoes.','The Ring of Fire.',['1 mark for the name Ring of Fire.']),
('Describe the distribution in this supplied record: 18 of 20 earthquakes lie close to mapped plate margins; two lie in plate interiors.','Most mapped earthquakes cluster near margins: 18 of 20, or 90%. The two interior events are exceptions. The pattern shows a strong margin association, not an exclusively margin-based distribution.',['1 mark for the dominant near-margin pattern; 1 for using the 18/20 evidence; 1 for retaining the two interior exceptions in the interpretation.']),
('Describe how sudden movement on a locked fault produces an earthquake.','Stress builds while the fault is locked despite wider movement. When the fault slips, stored strain energy is released. Seismic waves carry that energy and cause shaking.',['1 mark for the first stress-building step during locking; 1 for sudden slip and energy release; 1 for seismic waves and shaking as the result.']),
('Describe the hazard chain in this resource: an offshore fault suddenly lifts part of the seabed; coastal flooding follows; a bridge and water mains fail, isolating a settlement.','Seabed displacement can generate a tsunami and the recorded coastal flooding. Bridge failure interrupts access to the settlement. Broken water mains disrupt supply, adding a service problem to the physical damage.',['1 mark for linking seabed displacement to the flood/tsunami pattern as resource evidence; 1 for bridge failure and access using the observations; 1 for the distinct water-supply consequence supported by the record.']),
('Name the point on the surface vertically above the focus of an earthquake.','The epicentre.',['1 mark for the name epicentre.']),
('Name molten rock after it emerges at the Earth’s surface.','Lava.',['1 mark for the name lava.']),
('Name one measure that directly reduces a weak school building’s vulnerability to earthquake shaking.','Structural strengthening or retrofitting to suitable earthquake-resistant standards.',['1 mark for a relevant structural measure; warning messages alone are not a name for strengthening.']),
('How far do you agree that hazard information alone is sufficient to protect a community? A weak school remains occupied; officials provide warnings, but no strengthening, transport or shelter is funded. Judge protection against collapse and effective evacuation.','Disagree substantially. Warnings can alert people and support decisions, but cannot strengthen the weak school. Without transport, some residents may be unable to leave; without shelter, evacuation may not be sustainable. Information is useful, but structural protection and practical evacuation support are also needed for the specified dangers.',['Up to 4 marks for judgement of the given evidence: distinguish warning value from unchanged collapse risk; analyse transport and shelter limitations and their consequences. Do not credit unsupported general lists.','1 mark for a supported overall decision addressing sufficiency against the stated criterion; an implied but clear conclusion is acceptable.']),
('How far do you agree that warning drills should take priority over strengthening in this earthquake programme? The occupied school has weak walls; drills already occur each term; the funded retrofit meets an assessed structural weakness. Judge reduction of collapse deaths.','Disagree for the stated priority. Existing drills already support preparedness, while the weak walls leave a direct collapse risk. The assessed retrofit addresses that vulnerability in the occupied school. Drills remain useful, but further drills alone cannot repair the walls, so strengthening is the more direct priority for reducing collapse deaths in this setting.',['Up to 4 marks for applying the school evidence and judging the mechanism of each option against collapse deaths, including the value and limitation of drills.','1 mark for a supported prioritisation decision; accept another decision only with reasoning consistent with the supplied facts and criterion.']),
('How far do you agree that buying another monitoring instrument should take priority over evacuation today? A volcano’s activity is escalating; homes are inside the temporary exclusion area; safe transport and shelter are available. Judge immediate casualty reduction.','Disagree. More monitoring could improve information, but it would leave residents exposed inside the threatened area. Escalating activity and the exclusion advice support moving them, and transport and shelter make evacuation practicable. Evacuation is therefore the immediate priority, while monitoring should continue to inform later decisions.',['Up to 4 marks for judging the supplied activity/exposure evidence, monitoring’s information benefit and limitation, and the practical feasibility of evacuation.','1 mark for a supported decision tied to immediate casualty reduction; do not credit a claim that monitoring prevents eruption.'])
]

TASKS=[
{'objectives':['01','09','09-01'],'prompt':'''(a) Name the surface point vertically above an earthquake’s focus. [1]\n(b) Describe three stages connecting a locked fault to earthquake shaking. [3]\n(c) Name the Earth’s central metallic layer. [1]\n(d) Describe two observations in this record that support an internal-layer distinction: R is a thin solid outer rock layer, while T is metallic and central. [2]''','answer':'(a) Epicentre. (b) Stress builds during locking; sudden slip releases stored energy; seismic waves produce shaking. (c) Core. (d) R is an outer rocky layer whereas T is central; their rock-versus-metal composition also differs.','guide':['(a) 1 A mark for epicentre.','(b) 3 A marks: stress while locked, sudden slip/energy release, seismic-wave shaking; credit distinct stages.','(c) 1 A mark for core.','(d) 2 B marks for two interpreted resource contrasts: outer/central position and rock/metal material. No double credit for repeating the same point.'],'split':{'A':5,'B':2},'parts':[('a','Name',1,'A'),('b','Describe',3,'A'),('c','Name',1,'A'),('d','Describe',2,'B')]},
{'objectives':['04','05-02','08'],'prompt':'''A teaching resource records three settings. X: arrows converge; trench depth 7,500 m beside 4,000 m ocean floor; a slab descends beneath the other plate. Y: arrows separate; crust ages increase from 0 Ma at the centre to 10 and 20 Ma away on each side. Z: arrows slide sideways; earthquake locations follow the fault; no volcanic chain is recorded.\n(a) Name the margin type associated with sideways sliding. [1]\n(b) Describe the age pattern at Y and its tectonic significance. [3]\n(c) Describe two observations at X that support subduction. [2]\n(d) What judgement does the recorded evidence support about Z being free from earthquake risk merely because no volcanic chain is recorded? [1]''','answer':'(a) Conservative/transform. (b) Youngest crust is central; age increases outward on both sides from 0 to 10 to 20 Ma; this supports new-crust formation and spreading. (c) Converging arrows and the slab descending beneath the other plate; the deep trench is additional support. (d) No: the recorded earthquakes along the sliding fault contradict the claim.','guide':['(a) 1 A mark for conservative/transform.','(b) 3 B marks for the central-youngest feature, bilateral increase with values, and spreading interpretation from the data.','(c) 2 B marks for distinct resource evidence supporting subduction; accept the convergence, descending-slab and trench observations, maximum two.','(d) 1 C mark for a decision supported by the recorded earthquakes; no mark for an unsupported no.'],'split':{'A':1,'B':5,'C':1},'parts':[('a','Name',1,'A'),('b','Describe',3,'B'),('c','Describe',2,'B'),('d','What',1,'C')]},
{'objectives':['09','10','10-02'],'prompt':'''How far do you agree that a volcano exclusion zone alone is sufficient to protect residents? In a hypothetical settlement, monitoring indicates escalating activity and officials announce an exclusion area. Some households within it have no transport; the only shelter has no water supply. Judge whether residents can leave and remain safely outside the threatened area. [5]''','answer':'Disagree with sufficiency. The exclusion zone identifies where residents should not remain and can reduce exposure if they leave. However, households without transport may be unable to act on the warning, and shelter without water cannot support people adequately. Transport and a usable shelter with water are therefore necessary complements in this setting. Monitoring and a zone inform action but do not themselves move or accommodate residents.','guide':['Up to 4 C marks for judging relevant given evidence and consequences: zone’s exposure benefit, transport barrier, shelter/water limitation, and the role of complementary provision. Reward developed reasoning; do not credit the same point twice.','1 C mark for a supported overall decision about sufficiency against the stated evacuation criterion.'],'split':{'C':5},'parts':[('a','How far do you agree',5,'C')]}
]

